#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120711} :activity/id #uuid "8313edbb-dabc-5be6-9269-6a1622b71d3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436 :topic/id #uuid "f5218ca7-a9d3-5ad9-b17a-ee16b6e2b34a" :topic/math-academy-id 1805} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121049 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that a plane $\\Pi$ passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ and that the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle$ is perpendicular to the plane. Let's write down an equation of this plane using the dot product.\n\n![](images/0f/0f8353480099369ad92701d1799f72d46eab4bbe5c17add8cb37a4f34074311b.png)\n\nWe start by considering an arbitrary point $R(x,y,z)$ that lies on the plane. The position vectors of the points $R$ and $P$ are $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle$ respectively. So, the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =r-⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis parallel to the plane. Therefore, $\\overrightarrow{PR} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28b6770bd681257b1030679a421b80e3f3d1e8fbb86d2060b64c7301d0878c5c.png)\n\nNow, recall that if two nonzero vectors are perpendicular, then their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PR}\\perp \\mathbf{n} \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\overrightarrow{PR}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is called the **vector equation of the plane** that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}.$ In our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle) \\cdot \\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using the properties of the dot product, we can arrive at an alternative vector equation given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot n & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c = \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}$ is a (scalar) constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "77d83d72-9202-50d7-af28-72062f00bfa5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3255 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cf165cc4-ae6c-564d-b8e6-051c7aba1faa" :step/math-academy-id 17917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119640 :question/id #uuid "ba897e22-8104-5772-a0d3-9b062f3592f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4932"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f5c9c30-1745-5986-b116-e9e70a7c6161" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413464}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Vector"} :step/id #uuid "e689c57c-a11c-582d-bed2-1b5777ad003d" :step/math-academy-id 17918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119641 :question/id #uuid "a33f6c43-4e25-5d67-9f73-ca99a18fb434" :question/math-academy-id "e-4933"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0d93156-f9be-5e6e-976a-bef30dc892ee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420153}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Is Parallel To a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "36217cb6-2731-5a89-84ce-0738c511b417" :step/math-academy-id 17919 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119642 :question/id #uuid "43586a83-879c-5caf-9063-a8f4afbb8b4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4935"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413462}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line"} :step/id #uuid "1b13bc4d-57be-5d17-841a-4f2645bc3982" :step/math-academy-id 17921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121120 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane with the following normal vector: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nLet's use the scalar product to find a vector that is parallel to this plane.\n\nWe denote our parallel vector as $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle.$ Now, since $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find a nonzero solution to this equation. To do that, we solve the equation for *one* of the variables (it doesn't matter which one). For example, let's solve our equation for the variable $t_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the variable $t_1$ depends on $t_2$ and $t_3,$ which, in turn, can be *any* real numbers. The variables $t_2$ and $t_3$ are called **free variables**.\n\nHence, the general form of our vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 2t_2+t_3, \\, t_2, \\, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_2, t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTo find a particular vector parallel to the plane, we can substitute any values for $t_2$ and $t_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- For example, setting $t_2=0$ and $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1= \\langle 1, 0, 1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Or, we could set $t_2=1$ and $t_3=0$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\langle 2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n**Note:** There are infinitely many vectors parallel to a given plane. In our example, we found only two particular vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "980ef6f4-542d-5915-84b8-e34f4480ae68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6411 :tutorial/title "A Vector Parallel To a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "69d22458-d2d5-5c3d-b6fb-4714f2ea1b49" :step/math-academy-id 38733 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120713}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120016}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420157} :activity/id #uuid "77095dee-04b2-5198-bba9-43f589fc9964" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436 :topic/id #uuid "f5218ca7-a9d3-5ad9-b17a-ee16b6e2b34a" :topic/math-academy-id 1805} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493da2a3-c2b1-5d90-946c-32226dc19e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,-2⟩=⟨-3,4,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393045} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7fa3af-d391-569e-bc17-106f3e9396bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=⟨3,-5,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393046} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe2b282-7f73-5949-a7f3-cc5ddf1e2530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393047} {:answer/id #uuid "949e581b-c362-5470-828e-687788c962cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,-2⟩=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393048} {:answer/id #uuid "1521f0fb-a511-5bc0-893e-2c6a06cceff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393049} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ef96c8-9198-5e30-8832-aa55a71dc6d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ca2b2271-1ec0-5a3e-b5ac-687d91668797" :question/math-academy-id "q-70895" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,-5,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=3+4t \\\\ z=4-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,-5,-2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=3+4t \\\\ z=4-t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,3,4⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-3,4,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨3,-5,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6262b847-bfad-5541-b7d9-0c48d99eb024" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1066db60-12af-5e82-a7c0-d24afa450e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260024} {:answer/id #uuid "336d23d9-d848-54ca-86e6-782c1b13a369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260339} {:answer/id #uuid "6bfd83ea-5bb5-57e7-b814-e0e365ce1b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260820} {:answer/id #uuid "d4093fa7-7b24-5445-bf63-718de7a82e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261744} {:answer/id #uuid "f03309e1-ca15-5335-aab5-a2535a4337a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260339} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46674a6-7e2b-5656-b018-7f5bf5e7afa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa3bed00-1af4-5c46-8c12-264209634c51" :question/math-academy-id "q-127910" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,0,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩=\\sqrt{7}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩=\\sqrt{7}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-1,1,3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1} & ={t}_{2}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨3,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "6f1edd60-137c-5af0-bf08-f3f3e9df0344" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420156}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b182a09-cc9d-51c4-95fe-e78790ecf72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420142} {:answer/id #uuid "36cb63bc-ae79-51e4-b5ab-3039c42340e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420143} {:answer/id #uuid "4173b538-e381-5dd5-b0c5-10dc2eaa96c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420144} {:answer/id #uuid "502b671f-b1be-5d29-8f96-08571a87d173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420145} {:answer/id #uuid "72001519-6957-56f0-b511-f27a9ed3c85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420143} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a0d8dd-871f-5fc8-9699-7a78c5f80aac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3fa29ff6-5ae4-5642-82fd-03b1645e810c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127921" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨5,-1,3⟩=27.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨5,-1,3⟩=27$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨5,-1,3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨5,-1,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =5{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,5{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "7c175861-cf16-5bce-9a3d-7dd7700bfeb3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a8f836e-b1bd-5929-a0d4-cc62e2406547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-3,5⟩=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420147} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b6228-4151-5fb7-a8b8-4defb3112da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420148} {:answer/id #uuid "ab54b251-064b-57d4-8fc1-3a45c6f498e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-3,5⟩=⟨-2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420149} {:answer/id #uuid "b05ca4a9-4343-56a3-86ae-980793c8602f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩=⟨2,-3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420150} {:answer/id #uuid "f59eba3d-6e2f-504f-905a-a227d63c2c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-3,5⟩=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420148} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2947f04-3e78-507e-b09a-00ccebdf73ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee828cf7-8b80-575c-a644-51d699464b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-33037" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(2,-3,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(3,2,-8)$ and $B(1,2,-5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,-3,5⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(3,2,-8)$ and $B(1,2,-5)$, we can find a vector that is parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,2,-5⟩-⟨3,2,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-2,0,3⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d1b2442-6684-5f23-a6d5-881beb105644" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65e4dacb-b64f-5663-ae54-844ec9e37c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,4⟩=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260760} {:answer/id #uuid "a344d6d0-c843-57a0-87ed-6643487a6ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-2,2,1⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261298} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b752a4-52ea-54e2-96dc-81dc14098d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨4,-2,1⟩=18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261313} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9815dd-2b59-5495-91a4-c387e6882786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,-2⟩=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261371} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f71bee-c234-59c3-9e78-d739bf030e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261577} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e498358-75ca-5ef1-ae02-06dee6634086" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7885abf5-baa7-59dd-be20-bec3451c5be4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127956" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(4,-2,3)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨2,-2,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f1fe54dc-0b25-528c-81fb-caa7a89e418f"}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420141}]}
